<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Valmy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Cannonade of Valmy · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Valmy, fought on 20 September 1792 in Champagne in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, halted the Prussian-led invasion that threatened revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. French armies under Dumouriez and Kellermann held their ground in an artillery duel, and the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>'s army soon withdrew. The next day the newly assembled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> abolished the monarchy; Goethe, who was with the Prussian army, later wrote that a new era in the history of the world began that day.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle came early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a>, when the monarchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> sought to stop the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> or contain it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-ii-of-prussia">Frederick William II of Prussia</a>, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, sent the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> with a large army. Longwy fell on 23 August and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> on 2 September 1792. About 54,000 French troops in the Army of the North under Charles Dumouriez and the Army of the Centre under François Kellermann faced Brunswick's coalition army of about 84,000, which included Hessians and French royalist émigrés. Just over half of the French infantry were regulars of the old Royal Army, as were nearly all the cavalry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Dumouriez abandoned his plan to attack the Austrian Netherlands and approached the invaders from the rear. Kellermann joined him at Sainte-Menehould on 19 September, which put the French east of the Prussians and behind their lines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, troubled by bad weather and sickness, turned back to fight. On 20 September Kellermann took position on slopes near Valmy, around an old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmill</a> that he had razed, and his artillery opened what is called the Cannonade of Valmy.</p><p>Kellermann had about 36,000 men and 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> about 34,000 and 54 cannon. The French batteries proved superior, and the Prussian infantry made a cautious, fruitless advance under fire. Kellermann is said to have raised his hat and cried Vive la Nation, and the troops sang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ca-ira">Ça Ira</a>. To general surprise Brunswick broke off the action. Casualties were no more than three hundred French and two hundred Prussians. Brunswick withdrew towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 30 September without being attacked.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Historians have never settled why the Prussians withdrew. Most point to the strong French position, growing reinforcements and the unexpected spirit of the volunteers, together with Prussian worries about the Russian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Stories of a secret plea by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> or of bribery are doubted; Munro Price finds no convincing proof of bribery.</p><p>Beset by food shortages and dysentery, the Prussians retreated beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and French troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> in October. On the day of the battle the Legislative Assembly had transferred its power to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a>, which then abolished the monarchy and proclaimed the French Republic. Johann Wolfgang von Goethe, who was with the Prussian army, later wrote that a new epoch in the history of the world had begun. Kellermann was ennobled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1808 and became Duke of Valmy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Valmy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The invading armies were driven back after the <b>Battle of Valmy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a></li></ul></section>
